container/ -- build and test in a container (podman or docker)
==============================================================

Quick start (podman, from the top of this repository):

    podman build -t sun4v-qemu-kit-build -f container/Containerfile container
    podman run --rm --userns=keep-id -v "$PWD":/src sun4v-qemu-kit-build

The first command builds the image once. The second runs `make all` in it; the
result is ./output. Any other command goes at the end:

    podman run --rm --userns=keep-id -v "$PWD":/src sun4v-qemu-kit-build \
        make test-all
    podman run --rm --userns=keep-id -v "$PWD":/src sun4v-qemu-kit-build \
        make release

docker: the same without --userns=keep-id, with --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)".

What it is for: a host without the sparc64 cross toolchain, and building in a
clean room. The image holds the toolchain only; the source tree is mounted, so
the image does not change with the tree. Debian and Ubuntu bases only
(REQUIREMENTS.LINUX: the rpm distributions have no sparc64 cross glibc).

The login gate needs a guest image; without it that gate is skipped:

    -v /path/to/images:/images:ro -e GUEST_DISK=/images/<image>

`make bootstrap-check` needs the sparc binfmt_misc handlers; a container uses
the host's.


Other bases
-----------
One Containerfile, three bases. Ubuntu 24.04 is the default:

    base          gcc  qemu-user
    ------------  ---  ----------
    Ubuntu 24.04  13   qemu 8.2.2
    Debian 13     14   qemu 10.0
    Ubuntu 26.04  15   qemu 10.2

The others are build arguments:

    podman build -t sun4v-qemu-kit-build-debian13 \
        -f container/Containerfile container \
        --build-arg BASE=docker.io/library/debian:stable --build-arg GCCV=14
    podman build -t sun4v-qemu-kit-build-ubuntu2604 \
        -f container/Containerfile container \
        --build-arg BASE=docker.io/library/ubuntu:26.04 --build-arg GCCV=15 \
        --build-arg QEMU_USER=qemu-user

(26.04 has no qemu-user-static, hence QEMU_USER=qemu-user.) Then use the image
name in the `podman run` lines above.


Clean room
----------
container/clean-room.sh <image> [commit] [command...] makes a fresh clone of
this repository at a commit (default HEAD, committed content only), mounts it
as /src in a fresh container and opens a shell (or runs the command):

    container/clean-room.sh sun4v-qemu-kit-build-debian13
    container/clean-room.sh sun4v-qemu-kit-build-ubuntu2604 HEAD \
        sh -c './configure && make all test-all release-check'
    GUEST_DISK=/path/disk.s10hw2 container/clean-room.sh <image> HEAD \
        sh -c './configure && make all test-all release-check'

With no command: an interactive bash. GUEST_DISK is optional; without it the
login gate is skipped.

/log in the container is kept (under $CR, default
/tmp/sun4v-clean-room/<image>/), for logs: `make all 2>&1 | tee /log/all.log`.
Do not switch images in the same /src: build/toolchain-check.sh clears an
OUTDIR built by other tools, but a new clean-room clone is the cleaner way.
